  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a \nlow pressure trough moving from the Great Basin into the Rockies\nwith a distinct shortwave seen in the GOES-18 WV imagery in SRN\nNV/SW UT, and a closed upper-low in the Eastern Pacific near \n34N/140W. The southern periphery of the trough in the Great \nBasin/Rockies appears to be a tad farther south than progged \n24-hours ago, and thus will likely keep the strongest pressure \ngradients north of our neck of the woods today. Models seem to \nbe backing off on the breezy to locally windy westerly winds \ntoday, and this seems acceptable given the current trends. \nAlthough winds will be lower in general, we can still expect to \nsee elevated fire weather concern today. However, confidence is \nlow on any location actually getting up to critical levels \ntoday. \n\nThe closed upper-low in the Eastern Pacific is then expected to\nrapidly track east into central California by 24/21Z Friday \nafternoon, then amplify as it moves into the Desert Southwest \nthis weekend. The trough axis is expected to push through \nnorthern Arizona Sunday morning. The 22/12Z NBM Grand Ensemble\n(which includes the ECMWF/GEFS and GEPS ensembles) indicates a \n40-45kt jet plume at 700 mb moving into the western portions of \nSoutheast Arizona by 26/00Z Saturday afternoon and extending \nacross the SE half of Southeast Arizona at 26/12Z Sunday \nmorning. These stronger winds will likely make their way down to\nthe surface both Saturday and Sunday, with the 23/04Z NBM \n24-hour max wind gust for Saturday afternoon near 40 kts at \nKTUS/KOLS and KDUG. The 22/21Z Short Range Ensemble Forecast \n(SREF) probability for reaching critical fire weather conditions\n(RH<=15% and 20-foot wind speed=>20 mph) indicate a 30-50% from\nTucson east at 26/00Z Saturday afternoon with 70% along the \nArizona/New Mexico border. Confidence is building in not only a \nstronger wind both Saturday and Sunday across Southeast Arizona,\nbut a stronger confidence that we will hit critical fire \nweather conditions as well across Fire Weather Zone 152. \nAlthough the winds will still strong Sunday east of Tucson, \nincreasing RH values due to lower temperatures and increasing \nlower level moisture from the west will reduce the threat for \ncritical fire weather conditions.\n\nThere is decent agreement with the ensemble members of the GEFS\nand ECMWF replacing another upper-low under the ridge Sunday in\nthe wake of the one that will impact us...this one a bit farther\nsouth and west of the previous one. Models strengthen/amplify this\ntrough while importing deeper subtropical moisture toward \nArizona the middle of next week. If this moisture makes its way \ninto the area, it would be capable of supporting showers/ \nthunderstorms. However, my confidence remains medium at best \nthat we will see this plume advect into Southeast Arizona. \nPattern recognition tells me that it would be more likely get \nshunted east of our forecast area.",
